---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../seo/schema';

const origin = Astro.site ?? new URL('https://getaipractice.site');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About AI Practice: How the Lessons Are Made"
  description="How AI Practice is built: lesson standards, source policy, changing-fact reviews, local-only progress and vendor neutrality."
  structuredData={[
    breadcrumbJsonLd(
      [
        { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
        { name: 'About', path: '/about' },
      ],
      origin,
    ),
  ]}
>
  <h1>About this project</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A free, practice-first way to learn practical AI skills — built around short lessons and real
    exercises instead of video courses and prompt lists.
  </p>

  <h2>Who writes this</h2>
  <p>
    AI Practice is an independent, one-person project. It started as a personal collection of the
    checks that actually changed how I use AI at work — verifying claims, grounding answers in
    sources, keeping sensitive data out of chats — and turned into lessons with practice. There is
    no vendor to sell you anything and no course to upsell; the site stays model-agnostic because
    that is the only version of this advice that is honest.
  </p>

  <h2>How lessons are written</h2>
  <p>
    Every lesson follows one template: a short concept, a bad example and a better one, the mechanism
    that explains why it works, and then an exercise. Feedback explains what was good, what was
    missing and why it matters — a wrong answer is part of the lesson, not a penalty.
  </p>

  <h2>Practice without an account</h2>
  <p>
    Exercises are graded locally in your browser — no AI API is called and no account exists. Your
    progress (completed lessons, XP, streak) is stored on this device only; clearing your browser
    data clears it.
  </p>

  <h2>Facts that change</h2>
  <p>
    AI products move quickly, so content separates stable principles from product details.
    Product-specific claims carry a source and a review date, and anything that cannot be kept
    current is written as a principle instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Neutral by design</h2>
  <p>
    The site is model-agnostic: skills are taught so they transfer across tools, and no vendor pays
    for placement. There is no “best AI” list — only explanations of what a capability is good for.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Corrections are welcome. Fixes ship with the next deploy, and every product-specific fact is
    re-reviewed on a schedule — the facts registry records a review date for each one (next full
    pass: December 2026).
  </p>
  <!-- TODO(owner): add a public correction channel (email or issue tracker) when available. -->
</BaseLayout>
